WEBVTT

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:29.500
<v Ira Glass>For over 20 years, Eric Capstick's worked for Yankee Stadium. He's a camera operator. During the game, he runs around the crowd, looking for fans to put up on this impossibly huge TV screen that sits over the outfield. And I've seen lots of people work a crowd with a microphone or a camera over the years, and I can say definitively, he is masterful.
<v Ira Glass>Eric Capstick 在洋基体育场工作了 20 多年，他是一名摄像师。比赛期间，他奔波在人群中，寻找观众，把他们展示在外场上方那个巨大的电视屏幕上。多年来，我见过很多人拿着麦克风或摄像机活跃气氛，我可以肯定地说，他是大师级的。

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:39.370
<v Eric Capstick>So I'd appreciate it if you gave me some really great energy, like [GROWLS] you know, positive Yankee energy. Are you guys a bachelorette party or something?
<v Eric Capstick>所以我希望你们能展现出非常棒的活力，比如 [低吼] 你们懂的，积极的洋基精神。你们是单身派对之类的吗？

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:40.130
<v Women>Yeah.
<v Women>是的。

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:42.650
<v Eric Capstick>All right. I'm going to try and get you for the second shot.
<v Eric Capstick>好吧，我要试着在第二个镜头里拍你们。

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:43.250
<v Women>Yay.
<v Women>耶。

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:48.530
<v Ira Glass>He's this lanky, friendly guy. By the way, he seems to know everybody working in the stadium.
<v Ira Glass>他是一个瘦长、友好的家伙。顺便说一句，他似乎认识体育场里工作的每一个人。

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:49.290
<v Eric Capstick>Daphne and Curtis.
<v Eric Capstick>Daphne 和 Curtis。

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:50.000
<v Man>What's good?
<v Man>怎么了？

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.030
<v Eric Capstick>What's up, bro?
<v Eric Capstick>兄弟，怎么了？

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:55.220
<v Ira Glass>When he walks past, he tosses them pieces of bubblegum that he swiped from the Yankees dugout.
<v Ira Glass>当他经过时，他会扔给他们从洋基队休息区顺来的泡泡糖。

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:55.580
<v Eric Capstick>Danny.
<v Eric Capstick>Danny。

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.860
<v Ira Glass>A piece of gum arcs through the air.
<v Ira Glass>一块口香糖在空中划过弧线。

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:10.460
<v Eric Capstick>Oh, he dropped it. He dropped it. But usually, he has pretty good hands. I like to say that half the people in the building can't catch. It's pretty accurate, actually.
<v Eric Capstick>哦，他没接住。他没接住。但通常他的手感很好。我喜欢说楼里有一半人接不住东西。实际上，这很准确。

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:20.090
<v Ira Glass>The whole purpose of putting fans up on the big screen is to keep the crowd energized between innings, when nothing's happening on the field. So what Eric's looking for is pumped up, passionate fans.
<v Ira Glass>把观众放在大屏幕上的全部目的就是在局间休息、球场上没有比赛时保持观众的活力。所以 Eric 寻找的是充满激情、热情高涨的观众。

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:27.250
<v Eric Capstick>Sometimes fans ask for Kiss Cam. I'm like, no, you got to go to Queens for Kiss Cam. We don't do Kiss Cam here.
<v Eric Capstick>有时粉丝会要求 Kiss Cam（接吻镜头）。我心想，不，想看 Kiss Cam 得去皇后区。我们这里不做 Kiss Cam。

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:33.440
<v Ira Glass>You get that Queens reference? He's saying Kiss Cam's the kind of low-brow garbage that you see at Mets games.
<v Ira Glass>你明白那个关于皇后区的梗吗？他是说 Kiss Cam 那种低俗的玩意儿你在大都会队的比赛里才看得到。

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:40.420
<v Eric Capstick>How are you doing? Hi, I want to get the kids. I mean, you know, primarily, so you should sit, probably.
<v Eric Capstick>你们好吗？嗨，我想拍这些孩子。我是说，你知道，主要得拍他们，所以你们最好还是坐下。

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:46.760
<v Ira Glass>He's talking to some teachers and parents of kids from a school, PS 71 in the Bronx. They're here on a class trip.
<v Ira Glass>他正在和来自布朗克斯 71 小学的一些老师和孩子家长交谈。他们是来参加班级旅行的。

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.180
<v Eric Capstick>You don't want to be in it?
<v Eric Capstick>你不想出镜吗？

00:01:48.180 --> 00:02:01.800
<v Ira Glass>Eric likes to position himself between the people who he's shooting and the screen, so they can actually see themselves on the huge screen right over his shoulder. Sometimes that means perching himself over the edge of a balcony, but not this time.
<v Ira Glass>Eric 喜欢把自己定位在他拍摄的人和屏幕之间，这样他们就能在他肩膀上方的巨大屏幕上看到自己。有时这意味着他得骑在阳台边缘，但这次不用。

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.270
<v Eric Capstick>We're about 10 seconds away.
<v Eric Capstick>我们还有 10 秒钟。

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:08.520
<v Ira Glass>Everybody waits. It feels like a long time.
<v Ira Glass>每个人都在等待。感觉时间过了很久。

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.730
<v Eric Capstick>Here we go. You're up. Hey, you're [INAUDIBLE].
<v Eric Capstick>我们开始吧。轮到你们了。嘿，你们是 [听不清]。

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:13.680
<v Eric Capstick>[CHEERING]
<v Eric Capstick>[欢呼声]

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.530
<v Child>Cool!
<v Child>酷！

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.000
<v Ira Glass>Have you been on TV before?
<v Ira Glass>你以前上过电视吗？

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.020
<v Child>No.
<v Child>没有。

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.090
<v Ira Glass>So how was it?
<v Ira Glass>感觉怎么样？

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:25.020
<v Child>I don't know. Exciting, yeah.
<v Child>不知道。很刺激，是的。

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:28.380
<v Ira Glass>And so what's so fun about being on TV?
<v Ira Glass>上电视有什么好玩的？

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:35.730
<v Child 2>Like, you just get to see yourself on there, and it makes you feel famous.
<v Child 2>就像，你可以在那上面看到自己，让你感觉自己很有名。

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:38.850
<v Ira Glass>How did you know what to do when the camera went on you?
<v Ira Glass>当摄像机对准你时，你怎么知道该做什么？

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:44.210
<v Child 2>Well, I've seen people and what they do. Like, they wave and stuff.
<v Child 2>嗯，我见过别人怎么做。比如，他们挥手之类的。

00:02:44.210 --> 00:03:02.490
<v Ira Glass>Duh-- even if you're in fourth grade. The camera's a magic wand. Somebody points it at you, you know what to do. A few friends sitting near these kids-- Kristen, and Joanne, and Howie-- they saw Eric, and they called out to him that he should shoot them next because it was Joanne's birthday. Eric saw right through that. I didn't.
<v Ira Glass>废话——即使你是四年级学生也一样。摄像机就像一根魔杖。有人把它指向你，你就知道该做什么。这几个坐在孩子附近的朋友——Kristen、Joanne 和 Howie——他们看到了 Eric，并对他大喊，让他下一个拍他们，因为今天是 Joanne 的生日。Eric 一眼就看穿了，但我没看出来。

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:03.730
<v Ira Glass>It's your birthday?
<v Ira Glass>今天是你的生日？

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:07.320
<v Joanne>No, it's not my birthday. He lied because I wanted to be on the camera.
<v Joanne>不，不是我的生日。他撒谎是因为我想上镜。

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.210
<v Ira Glass>Why do you want to be on the camera?
<v Ira Glass>你为什么要上镜？

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:14.280
<v Joanne>I don't know. Because I love the Yankees, and I'm happy to be here. And I want all my friends to be on TV.
<v Joanne>不知道。因为我爱洋基队，我很高兴能来到这里。而且我希望我所有的朋友都能上电视。

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.960
<v Ira Glass>Kristen and her friend was actually on the big screen once.
<v Ira Glass>Kristen 和她的朋友确实上过一次大屏幕。

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:27.540
<v Kristen>Oh my god, it's so exciting. People saw me on TV, and they texted me from home [INAUDIBLE]. We see you on camera. It was my moment of fame. I've never been on TV before. I was on TV.
<v Kristen>天哪，太令人兴奋了。人们在电视上看到了我，他们从家里给我发短信 [听不清]。我们在镜头里看到你了。这是我成名的时刻。我以前从没上过电视。我上电视了。

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:34.300
<v Joanne>Well, we actually were on TV once last-- two seasons ago. There was a ball that came out, and our friend--
<v Joanne>其实我们上过一次电视，是在……两个赛季前。当时有一个球飞出来，我们的朋友……

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:34.800
<v Kristen>[INAUDIBLE]
<v Kristen>[听不清]

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.870
<v Howie>[INAUDIBLE] first home run, and our friend caught the ball and dropped it.
<v Howie>[听不清] 第一个本垒打，我们的朋友接住了球，但又掉了。

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:51.950
<v Joanne>And it fell right on our seats, and we all bent over to get it. And then we watched SportsCenter that night, and you saw all of us like, ahh, and bent over. And that's our one time we were on TV.
<v Joanne>它直接掉在我们的座位上，我们都弯下腰去捡。那天晚上我们看了 SportsCenter 节目，你看到我们所有人都在那里“啊”，弯着腰。那是我们唯一一次上电视。

00:03:51.950 --> 00:04:06.690
<v Ira Glass>So they were on SportsCenter-- SportsCenter. It's so far away in the stands, so small on the screen, that nobody they knew recognized them. Nobody texted, nobody called. Yet still, it's kind of satisfying.
<v Ira Glass>所以他们上了 SportsCenter——SportsCenter。他们在看台上离得太远，在屏幕上太小，以至于他们认识的人都没认出他们。没人发短信，没人打电话。然而，这仍然让人感到满足。

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:22.880
<v Joanne>Like I DVRed SportsCenter and then paused it and took pictures of the TV. And every year it shows up in my Facebook memories, I share it. I'm like, oh, that's when Wayne blew the ball. [LAUGHS]
<v Joanne>我把 SportsCenter 录了下来，暂停并拍了电视的照片。每年它都会出现在我的 Facebook 回忆里，我就会分享它。我说，噢，那就是 Wayne 漏掉那个球的时候。[笑声]

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:42.940
<v Ira Glass>You're only on the big screen at the stadium for a few seconds. But to get there, one woman told me, it makes your day. Another said she was recognized on the subway on the way home from a game that she had been on the screen. It's like this thrilling thing the Yankees can bestow on any lucky fan. So much better than a bobblehead. Because who doesn't want to get on TV?
<v Ira Glass>你在体育场的大屏幕上只出现几秒钟。但正如一位女士告诉我的，这能让你这一天过得很开心。另一位说她在回家的地铁上被认出来了，因为她出现在屏幕上。这就像洋基队可以赐予任何幸运粉丝的一件令人兴奋的事情。比摇头娃娃好多了。因为谁不想上电视呢？

00:04:42.940 --> 00:05:13.960
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, non-TV people suddenly find themselves on TV. They didn't expect it. They didn't ask for it. They just found out they were, whether they wanted it or didn't want it. And some definitely did not want it. Others didn't even know it was happening because they're babies. What that kind of attention can do to you. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，非电视界人士突然发现自己上了电视。他们没预料到，也没要求。他们只是发现自己上镜了，不管他们想不想。有些人绝对不想上。有些人甚至不知道发生了什么，因为他们还是婴儿。这种关注会对你产生什么影响。来自 WBEZ 芝加哥，这是《美国生活》。我是 Ira Glass。请继续收听。

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:28.870
<v Ira Glass>Act I. Anything You Say Can and Will Be Used on Television. So since the 1980s, there's been a way that tons of ordinary Americans suddenly found themselves on TV, though it probably was not how they wanted to be making their TV debuts.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。你说的任何话都可能并在将来会被用于电视。自 20 世纪 80 年代以来，数以百万计的普通美国人突然发现自己上了电视，尽管这可能不是他们想要的电视首秀方式。

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:29.670
<v Ira Glass>[SIRENS]
<v Ira Glass>[警笛声]

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:34.840
<v Man>Stop. Stop. Stop. OK.
<v Man>停。停。停。好吧。

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:50.580
<v Ira Glass>So if you live in this country, you probably know the TV show Cops. This particular clip is from a kind of updated version of Cops called Live PD, which is made by a different company. It's actually more popular than Cops these days. And with me to discuss this is Dan Taberski. And Dan, you have watched a lot of these shows over the years.
<v Ira Glass>如果你住在这个国家，你可能知道电视节目《Cops》（警察）。这个片段来自《Cops》的一个升级版，叫《Live PD》，是由另一家公司制作的。如今它实际上比《Cops》更受欢迎。和我一起讨论这件事的是 Dan Taberski。Dan，多年来你看了很多这样的节目。

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:55.200
<v Dan Taberski>I have. I have watched a lot of Cops. I've watched a lot of Cops over the years-- too much.
<v Dan Taberski>是的。这些年来我看了很多《Cops》。我看过很多——太多了。

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:02.270
<v Ira Glass>And I know that one of the things that's interested you about that show and that you enjoy when you watch a show, it comes from the fact that you yourself used to make television.
<v Ira Glass>我知道你对那个节目的兴趣，以及你在观看节目时的享受，源于你自己也曾经制作过电视节目。

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:23.850
<v Dan Taberski>Yeah, I did for a long time. And a lot of reality shows, too, mostly kid shows and game shows, silly stuff. But I know what tricks that you employ in making a reality show. And I've always wondered, how were they doing this? What are the tricks that they're using? What's the difference between what they're showing me on TV versus reality?
<v Dan Taberski>是的，我做了很久。还有很多真人秀，大多是儿童节目和游戏节目，些愚蠢的东西。但我知道制作真人秀时所用的技巧。我一直很好奇，他们是怎么做到的？他们用了什么招数？他们在电视上展示的内容与现实有什么区别？

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:24.830
<v Ira Glass>Like, what really happened?
<v Ira Glass>比如，到底发生了什么？

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:25.980
<v Dan Taberski>Yeah, yeah.
<v Dan Taberski>是的，没错。

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:42.040
<v Ira Glass>And you have a new podcast about this very question. It's called Running from Cops. I've listened to the whole thing. It's a great show. And in this show, you take that question apart for Cops and for Live PD. And I think before we get further, you should explain what Live PD is for the people who haven't seen it.
<v Ira Glass>你有一个关于这个问题的播客。叫《Running from Cops》（逃离警察）。我听完了整部节目。这是一个很棒的节目。在这个节目中，你拆解了《Cops》和《Live PD》的这个问题。我想在我们深入探讨之前，你应该为那些没看过的人解释一下什么是《Live PD》。

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:57.040
<v Dan Taberski>Yeah, the thing about Live PD is that half the country doesn't even know what it is. And in the meantime, it was the most DVRed show of 2018. It's three hours long of live policing on Friday night and then another three hours of live policing on Saturday night.
<v Dan Taberski>是的，《Live PD》的情况是，半个国家甚至不知道它是什么。但同时，它是 2018 年被录制次数最多的节目。它有 3 个小时的周五晚直播警务，还有 3 个小时的周六晚直播警务。

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:05.910
<v Ira Glass>So it is a lot of hours of television. And just so people can picture the show, describe what Live PD looks like, what makes it different from Cops.
<v Ira Glass>所以这是一个非常长的电视节目。为了让人们想象一下这个节目，描述一下《Live PD》看起来是什么样子的，它与《Cops》有什么不同。

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:26.550
<v Dan Taberski>Live PD is mainly different because it's live. It's basically set up like any SBN show. So there's a host, and there's all these monitors behind him. And the monitors are following six to eight police departments around the country live. And he just keeps cutting to different police departments to show you the highlights of what's going on right now.
<v Dan Taberski>《Live PD》主要是因为它是在直播。它基本像任何体育直播节目一样设置。有一个主持人，他身后有很多监视器。监视器直播跟随全国六到八个警察局。他不断切换到不同的警察局，向你展示正在发生的事情的亮点。

