00:08:46.840 --> 00:09:20.710
<v Dan Taberski>So for police departments wanting to get their side out in this atmosphere, Live PD is great on TV and also social media. Unlike Cops, which is sort of old school, Live PD has a big social media presence. It's a live show. People tweet along live. They have a rabid fan base. During the show, the police departments will actually tweet, if you liked seeing Pasco County, Florida, for example, on Live PD, here's how you can apply to become an officer yourself. So I spoke to this one guy, Sheriff Ozzie Knezovich. He's the sheriff in Spokane, Washington. And that's one of the places where Live PD's been filming.
<v Dan Taberski>所以对于想要在这种氛围中传达自己立场的警察局来说，《Live PD》在电视和社交媒体上都很棒。不像《Cops》那种老派做法，《Live PD》拥有巨大的社交媒体存在感。这是一个直播节目。人们实时发推文互动。他们拥有狂热的粉丝群。节目期间，警察局甚至会发推文，如果你喜欢在《Live PD》上看到佛罗里达州帕斯科县，这里有你可以申请成为警官的方式。所以我采访了这位警长 Ozzie Knezovich。他是华盛顿州斯波坎市的警长。那是《Live PD》拍摄的地点之一。

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:32.460
<v Ozzie Knezovich>This thing has tapped into something that I can't explain. I've never seen anything like it before-- not in 28 years. I can tell you that Live PD has done wonders for our recruiting.
<v Ozzie Knezovich>这东西触及了一些我无法解释的东西。我从未见过这样的事情——在我这 28 年里。我可以告诉你，《Live PD》对我们的招募工作创造了奇迹。

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.230
<v Dan Taberski>Really? How?
<v Dan Taberski>真的吗？怎么做到的？

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:38.210
<v Ozzie Knezovich>Applications-- people calling us all over the country.
<v Ozzie Knezovich>申请——人们从全国各地打电话给我们。

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:38.700
<v Dan Taberski>Really?
<v Dan Taberski>真的吗？

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:46.230
<v Ozzie Knezovich>Yes. It's been amazing. It's been amazing the amount of Christmas cards we got from people all over the country.
<v Ozzie Knezovich>是的。这太惊人了。我们从全国各地收到那么多的圣诞卡，真是令人惊奇。

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:47.310
<v Dan Taberski>Get out of here.
<v Dan Taberski>别开玩笑了。

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:57.980
<v Ozzie Knezovich>No. It shows people at their worst. Well, no, it shows what we're dealing with. It shows what society is dealing with.
<v Ozzie Knezovich>不。它展示了人们最糟糕的一面。好吧，不，它展示了我们正在处理的事情。它展示了社会正在处理的事情。

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:12.520
<v Ira Glass>So one of the things that's the most interesting about your series, Running from Cops, is that you really do try to figure out what is the gap between what the programs show us and what really happened out on the street. And as part of that, you and your team watched how many hours?
<v Ira Glass>你系列节目《Running from Cops》最有趣的地方之一，就是你真的试图找出节目展示给我们的内容与街头实际发生的事情之间的差距。作为其中的一部分，你和你的团队看了多少个小时？

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:15.380
<v Dan Taberski>846 episodes of Cops.
<v Dan Taberski>846 集《Cops》。

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:16.420
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:19.390
<v Dan Taberski>And for Live PD, we watched hundreds of hours.
<v Dan Taberski>对于《Live PD》，我们看了数百个小时。

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:22.510
<v Ira Glass>And the 846 episodes of Cops, why 846?
<v Ira Glass>那 846 集《Cops》，为什么是 846？

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:31.760
<v Dan Taberski>That's all we can get. [LAUGHS] That is what was available to us. There have been over 1,000, and we could get 846.
<v Dan Taberski>那是我们能拿到的全部。[笑声] 这就是我们能获取的资源。已经有超过 1000 集了，我们只能拿到 846 集。

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:48.580
<v Ira Glass>And so now, what we've done is we've asked you to do an excerpt from your series here for us, where you try to figure out the gap between what we see on these two programs and what really happened, and where you talk to the people getting arrested on Live PD and on Cops about the experience of being reluctantly put on television.
<v Ira Glass>所以现在，我们请你在这里为我们做个节选，你试图找出这两个节目中我们看到的与实际发生的事情之间的差距，以及你采访那些在《Live PD》和《Cops》中被逮捕的人，关于被迫上电视的体验。

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:49.300
<v Dan Taberski>Right.
<v Dan Taberski>没错。

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:55.820
<v Ira Glass>And so what's going to happen now is you're going to start with Cops and then go to Live PD. Take it away.
<v Ira Glass>所以现在会发生的是，你将从《Cops》开始，然后转到《Live PD》。请开始吧。

00:10:55.820 --> 00:11:12.030
<v Dan Taberski>We'll start here. This is Cops, 2013, season 26, episode 14. This scene happens in Gwinnett County, Georgia. It's after midnight, and an officer pulls up on two teenagers. They're parked in a church parking lot.
<v Dan Taberski>我们从这里开始。这是《Cops》，2013 年，第 26 季，第 14 集。这一幕发生在佐治亚州格温内特县。午夜过后，一名警官截停了两个青少年。他们停在一个教堂的停车场里。

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:13.060
<v Officer>Hey, how are you doing?
<v Officer>嘿，你们好吗？

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:14.230
<v Teenager 1>Great, how are you?
<v Teenager 1>很好，你呢？

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:15.610
<v Officer>Good. What are you guys doing here?
<v Officer>很好。你们在这里做什么？

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:16.380
<v Teenager 2>Just hanging out.
<v Teenager 2>只是待着。

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:21.230
<v Officer>Just hanging out? OK, well, it's the church, and it's closed.
<v Officer>只是待着？好吧，这是教堂，而且已经关门了。

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:22.920
<v Teenager 2>Are we not supposed to be here? OK.
<v Teenager 2>我们不应该在这里吗？好吧。

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:24.990
<v Officer>So you guys have your IDs with you?
<v Officer>所以你们带身份证了吗？

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:26.200
<v Teenager 2>I've got mine.
<v Teenager 2>我有我的。

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:28.000
<v Officer>Anything illegal, drugs, anything like that at all?
<v Officer>有非法物品吗，毒品，之类的东西吗？

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:28.400
<v Teenager 3>No.
<v Teenager 3>没有。

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:32.050
<v Officer>No? OK. Would you mind if I check to make sure there's nothing illegal in the car?
<v Officer>没有吗？好吧。你介意我检查一下车里没有非法物品吗？

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:32.490
<v Teenager 2>Yeah.
<v Teenager 2>好吧。

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:33.730
<v Officer>OK.
<v Officer>行。

00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:42.430
<v Dan Taberski>The guy tells the officer that he's out on bond for possession of cocaine, but that he's been clean for a month. So now we're all getting suspicious, right?
<v Dan Taberski>那个人告诉警官他因为持有可卡因正在保释期，但他已经一个月没碰了。所以现在我们都起疑心了，对吧？

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:43.300
<v Officer>Just leave it there for now.
<v Officer>暂时放在那儿就行。

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:53.380
<v Dan Taberski>The cop starts rooting around in the front seat, past the half-drunk soda cups and empty chip bags on the floorboard. And pretty much immediately, he finds what he is looking for.
<v Dan Taberski>警察开始在副驾驶座翻找，越过地板上喝了一半的苏打水杯和空薯片袋。他几乎立刻找到了他要找的东西。

00:11:53.380 --> 00:12:02.860
<v Officer>This right here, crack cocaine has almost like a cake consistency to it. So we're going to nick test a portion of that and see-- test a small amount.
<v Officer>这个，可卡因的质地几乎像蛋糕一样。所以我们要取一部分做一个快速测试，看看——测试一小部分。

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:08.830
<v Dan Taberski>The officer picks up a sample, and he does what's called a nick test, a roadside drug test.
<v Dan Taberski>警官取了一个样本，做了所谓的“尼克测试”，即路边毒品测试。

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:25.520
<v Officer>If it ends up being cocaine, it'll have a blue or a blue over pink change to it. And that would be cocaine. The blue hue on the pink on the bottom, that's positive for cocaine.
<v Officer>如果最终是可卡因，它会变成蓝色，或者蓝色变粉色。那就是可卡因。底部粉色上的蓝色色调，说明可卡因检测呈阳性。

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:32.440
<v Dan Taberski>The guy and the girl look stunned. They deny the cocaine is theirs. The cop arrests them both.
<v Dan Taberski>那个男人和女孩看起来惊呆了。他们否认可卡因是他们的。警察逮捕了他们俩。

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:37.170
<v Officer>You're being placed under arrest for possession of cocaine. Just go ahead and place your hands behind your back, OK?
<v Officer>你因持有可卡因被捕。继续，把你的手放在背后，好吗？

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:47.650
<v Dan Taberski>And scene. It goes to commercial.
<v Dan Taberski>场面结束。进入广告。

00:12:47.650 --> 00:13:11.980
<v Dan Taberski>Now I know my way around a reality show edit room. And as far as reality shows go, Cops seems pretty real. In that segment we just watched, there's no music. There's no narrator. It's just edited-down observational filming of police at work. That's what I like about it. And it's that style that makes it so believable to the casual viewer. It is what it is-- until it's not.
<v Dan Taberski>现在我知道真人秀剪辑室是怎么回事了。就真人秀而言，《Cops》看起来非常真实。在我们刚才看的那个片段中，没有音乐。没有旁白。它只是经过剪辑的警察工作纪实拍摄。这就是我喜欢它的原因。正是这种风格让它在普通观众眼中如此可信。这就是事实——直到它不再是为止。

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:15.980
<v Dan Taberski>Did you always want to be a police officer? Was that always the plan?
<v Dan Taberski>你一直想当警察吗？一直都有这个计划吗？

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:18.700
<v John Burdges>I don't know. It seemed like a good idea at the time.
<v John Burdges>不知道。当时觉得这是个好主意。

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:23.530
<v Dan Taberski>Really? It would never seem like a good idea to me. It seems dangerous, and difficult-- and thankless, often.
<v Dan Taberski>真的吗？对我来说这永远不会是个好主意。这看起来很危险，很难——而且经常是吃力不讨好的。

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:26.620
<v John Burdges>It is dangerous, and it is difficult, and it's very thankless.
<v John Burdges>确实很危险，确实很难，而且非常吃力不讨好。

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:43.290
<v Dan Taberski>This is John Burdges. He's a former narcotics officer, and he loved it. But after 14 years on the job, he went to law school. He's been a defense attorney ever since here in Buford, Georgia, where he was retained by that young woman in that Cops episode that we just heard.
<v Dan Taberski>这是 John Burdges。他曾是一名缉毒警察，他很喜欢那份工作。但在那份工作干了 14 年后，他去读了法学院。从那以后他一直是佐治亚州布福德的一名辩护律师，他受聘于我们刚才听到的那个《Cops》剧集里的年轻女子。

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:58.810
<v John Burdges>I thought she was guilty, having been in law enforcement. And I'm very fond of the police, and I'm very pro-police. But I just thought it was a regular episode. So did her family, and so did the district attorney, and so did everybody else.
<v John Burdges>作为执法人员，我以为她有罪。而且我很喜欢警察，我也非常支持警察。但我只是以为那是一集普通的节目。她的家人也是这么想的，地区检察官也是这么想的，每个人都这么想。

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:04.900
<v Dan Taberski>Until, that is, a secondary test on that drug sample taken on the scene came back from the lab.
<v Dan Taberski>直到，在现场提取的毒品样本的二次检测从实验室传回来。

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:14.290
<v John Burdges>The alleged cocaine came back negative from the state lab that it was not cocaine. In fact, it was nothing. They couldn't figure out what it was. But it was certainly not a drug.
<v John Burdges>所谓的毒品从州实验室传回的结果是阴性，它不是可卡因。实际上，它什么都不是。他们搞不清那是什么。但可以肯定不是毒品。

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:45.080
<v Dan Taberski>In fact, that roadside drug test in that episode, they do them all the time on Cops. The colors tell you if it's coke or not. Easy-peasy. But what they never say on Cops, and which I didn't know myself, until now, is that, in fact, those tests, pretty much every jurisdiction in the country says they are inadmissible at trial, including Gwinnett County, Georgia, where that young woman and her boyfriend were arrested. But when that test pops up as blue on Cops, they never say, well, we'll see how the official test comes back. They say--
<v Dan Taberski>事实上，那一集中路边的毒品测试，他们在《Cops》里一直这样做。颜色告诉你是不是可卡因。很简单。但《Cops》里从来没说，我自己直到现在才知道，实际上在全国几乎所有的司法管辖区，这些测试在审判中是不可采信的，包括那个年轻女子和她男友被捕的佐治亚州格温内特县。但当测试在《Cops》上显示为蓝色时，他们从不说明，“好吧，让我们看看官方检测结果会怎样”。他们说——

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:47.140
<v Officer>And that would be cocaine.
<v Officer>那会是可卡因。

00:14:47.140 --> 00:15:21.850
<v Dan Taberski>But it wasn't. And that's not the only discrepancy that John Burdges learned about as this case progressed. The cop didn't just open the car door and find the suspected drugs, like on the show. He searched for 14 minutes before testing anything. And he didn't just do one test, like they show in that episode. He actually tested it two times for coke before it came back blue. One month after the official test results showed it wasn't cocaine, the producers of Cops aired that episode anyway, attracting the wrong kind of notoriety for Burgess' client.
<v Dan Taberski>但它不是。那并不是 John Burdges 在案件进展过程中了解到的唯一差异。警察并没有像节目中那样直接打开车门找到疑似毒品。他在测试任何东西之前搜索了 14 分钟。而且他也没有像节目中展示的那样只做了一次测试。实际上他测试了两次可卡因才变成蓝色。在官方检测结果显示它不是可卡因的一个月后，《Cops》的制作人还是播出了那一集，给 Burgess 的当事人带来了错误的名誉损失。

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:44.220
<v John Burdges>And in fact, she ended up, over a period of a few months, moving out of Gwinnett County, where she grew up, several miles away. You put this young lady through this for what? To film a damn TV show. And nobody cares.
<v John Burdges>事实上，在接下来的几个月里，她最终搬离了她长大的格温内特县，搬到了几英里外。你为了什么让这位年轻女士经历这些？为了拍个该死的电视节目。而且根本没人关心。

00:15:44.220 --> 00:16:08.470
<v Dan Taberski>Burgess's client didn't want to talk to me. She's trying to put the whole thing behind her. But why would she have agreed to be on Cops in the first place? Because the thing is for pretty much any reality show, Cops included, the producers do need your consent to show your face on their show, usually by signing a consent form. Why would anyone who just got arrested sign that piece of paper?
<v Dan Taberski>Burgess 的当事人不想跟我谈。她正试图把这一切抛在脑后。但她当初为什么要同意上《Cops》呢？因为问题在于对于几乎任何真人秀节目，包括《Cops》，制作人确实需要你的同意才能在节目中展示你的脸，通常是通过签署同意书。为什么刚被捕的人会签那张纸？

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:14.080
<v John Langley>Well, you got to talk to people, and you got to persuade them. Any producer that is worth a salt knows that.
<v John Langley>好吧，你必须和人们交谈，你必须说服他们。任何有价值的制作人都知道这一点。

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:22.980
<v Dan Taberski>John Langley is the co-creator of Cops. 30 years later, he still produces the show, now with the help of his son, Morgan.
<v Dan Taberski>John Langley 是《Cops》的联合创始人。30 年后，他仍然在制作这个节目，现在是在他儿子 Morgan 的帮助下。

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:28.500
<v John Langley>Most people who sign do so fully cognizant of what they're doing.
<v John Langley>大多数签署的人都是充分意识到自己在做什么。

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:30.900
<v Morgan>It's just like, they talk to him and listen--
<v Morgan>就像，他们和他谈话，听着——

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:46.120
<v John Langley>Here's the good news. At this stage of our show's life, people know the show, and it has a certain pop cultural iconic value. And they'll say, get those news cameras away from me. Say, we're with the Cops crew, and they go, oh, Cops. Well, that's OK.
<v John Langley>这是个好消息。在我们节目生涯的这个阶段，人们知道这个节目，它具有一定的流行文化标志性价值。他们会说，让那些新闻摄像机离我远点。说，我们是《Cops》剧组，他们会说，哦，《Cops》。嗯，那没关系。

00:16:46.120 --> 00:17:02.030
<v Dan Taberski>I've actually heard Langley say this before, that people want to be reality show famous, even if it's Cops famous. And I've always found it hard to believe, but you kind of gotta take his word for it, unless you're going to track down the people who have been filmed by Cops and ask them yourself. And so we did.
<v Dan Taberski>我确实听过 Langley 这么说过，人们想出名，即使是《Cops》式的出名。我一直觉得难以置信，但除非你去追踪那些被《Cops》拍摄的人并亲自询问，否则你只能听他的。所以我们就这么做了。

00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:08.640
<v Corey Robinson>Because I was given a choice. Sign if I go to jail. I don't feel like the TV show should have anything to do with my freedom.
<v Corey Robinson>因为我被给了个选择。如果不签就得坐牢。我觉得电视节目不应该和我的自由有任何关系。

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:26.080
<v Dan Taberski>Corey Robinson was chased and filmed by Cops on his 18th birthday, while he was hanging out in a park in Tampa, Florida. And he says when he was sitting in the back of a police car, the first person to approach him wasn't a cop, but rather a producer from Cops, the show, who told him--
<v Dan Taberski>Corey Robinson 在他 18 岁生日那天在佛罗里达州坦帕的一个公园里游荡时被《Cops》追捕并拍摄。他说当他坐在警车后座时，第一个靠近他的人不是警察，而是《Cops》的制作人，他告诉他——

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:31.800
<v Corey Robinson>I need you to sign this release form, or you're going to jail with a felony trespassing.
<v Corey Robinson>我需要你签这份授权书，否则你就要因为重罪非法入侵被关进监狱。

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:37.440
<v Dan Taberski>So Corey says no way. And he asks to talk to an actual cop. And he says to the officer--
<v Dan Taberski>所以 Corey 说绝不。他要求和真正的警察谈谈。他对警官说——

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:52.450
<v Corey Robinson>They're telling me you're charging me with a felony trespassing, and I'm going to jail if I don't sign a release form. He was like, yeah, you should take him up on his offer. He's trying to help you. You need to sign the paper, or you go to jail. He put the window up, and he walked away.
<v Corey Robinson>他们告诉我你正在指控我重罪非法入侵，如果我不签授权书我就要坐牢。他说，是的，你应该接受他的提议。他在试图帮助你。你需要签那张纸，否则你就去坐牢。他关上窗户，走开了。

00:17:52.450 --> 00:18:11.710
<v Dan Taberski>In fact, of the nine people we were able to find who had been filmed by Cops, all but one say they didn't sign a release, were too drunk or high to do it willingly, or they were coerced into it, like Corey Robinson. And that young woman in that video that I played you, the cocaine bust that turned out to not be cocaine--
<v Dan Taberski>事实上，在我们找到的九个被《Cops》拍摄的人中，除了一人之外，所有人都说他们没签授权书，要么喝醉了或磕了药无法自愿去做，要么像 Corey Robinson 一样被强迫去做。还有视频里的那个年轻女子，那个最终证明不是可卡因的可卡因抓捕——

00:18:11.710 --> 00:18:13.840
<v Officer>Does that not look like cocaine?
<v Officer>那看起来不像可卡因吗？

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:15.060
<v Teenager>What is that?
<v Teenager>那是什么？

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:16.440
<v Officer>Cocaine.
<v Officer>可卡因。

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:18.780
<v Dan Taberski>According to her attorney, John Burdges--
<v Dan Taberski>根据她的律师 John Burdges 的说法——

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:39.810
<v John Burdges>They told her, you either sign this waiver, or you won't get a bond. So you're sitting in jail in a holding area. You're 18 years old, and you can't get out of jail unless you sign this piece of paper. So yeah, she signed a waiver, but it was not voluntary. Nothing's voluntary when you got handcuffs on.
<v John Burdges>他们告诉她，要么签这份弃权书，要么就得不到保释。所以你坐在监狱的拘留区里。你 18 岁，除非签了这张纸，否则你出不去。所以是的，她签了弃权书，但这并非出于自愿。当你被戴上手铐时，没有什么是自愿的。

00:18:39.810 --> 00:19:02.080
<v Dan Taberski>The producers of Cops maintain that they don't coerce subjects to sign consent forms and that they won't even consider a segment for the show unless they have a signed release in hand. A spokesperson told us sometimes people sign, then regret it, and make, quote, "outrageous allegations." So that's Cops. There's more going on than what you see on screen.
<v Dan Taberski>《Cops》的制作人坚称他们不会强迫拍摄对象签署同意书，而且除非他们手头有签署的授权书，否则他们甚至不会考虑把拍摄片段放进节目。一位发言人告诉我们，有时人们签了，然后后悔了，就做出所谓的“令人震惊的指控”。所以这就是《Cops》。屏幕上看到的比实际发生的要复杂得多。

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:34.350
<v Dan Taberski>But what about Live PD? I mean, it's live. How do you manipulate live? Nothing is more it is what it is than a live TV show, right? No one from Live PD would talk to us about the show or how it's produced. And so we set out to find the answer ourselves by tracking down suspects who have been on the show and having conversations about what actually ended up on television. In the past three years, they've shot in over 30 cities. And so we picked one-- Spokane, Washington.
<v Dan Taberski>但是《Live PD》呢？我是说，它是直播。你怎么操控直播？没有比现场直播节目更真实的东西了，对吧？没有《Live PD》的人愿意和我们谈论节目及其制作方式。所以我们通过追踪在该节目中出现过的嫌疑人，并就最终在电视上播出的内容进行对话，试图自己找出答案。在过去三年里，他们在 30 多个城市拍摄。所以我们挑选了一个——华盛顿州斯波坎市。

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:46.090
<v Man>Welcome back. Let's go to Spokane County, Washington. Deputy van Patton is there. And oh, no, looks like we've got another shirtless dude.
<v Man>欢迎回来。让我们去华盛顿州斯波坎县。副警长 van Patton 在那里。噢，不，看起来我们又有另一个打赤膊的家伙了。

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:58.740
<v Dan Taberski>Spokane's 89% white. And like a lot of places, they've got a pretty serious opioid problem. The city's been on 99 hours of Live PD. And so me and my producers, we watched them all.
<v Dan Taberski>斯波坎 89% 是白人。像许多地方一样，他们有相当严重的阿片类药物问题。这个城市在《Live PD》上出现了 99 个小时。我和我的制作人看完了所有的内容。

00:19:58.740 --> 00:20:00.370
<v Officer>Meth or heroin, James?
<v Officer>海洛因还是冰毒，James？

00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:01.260
<v James>Not doing anything.
<v James>什么也没做。

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:04.060
<v Officer>All right, James, you have the right to remain silent.
<v Officer>好的，James，你有权保持沉默。

00:20:04.060 --> 00:20:24.210
<v Dan Taberski>The goal was to look for clues as to the suspect's identity. They only say their first names, if even that. So we look for a first name, or a street sign maybe, so that we could see where the arrest happened. And then we would cross-reference that with court records from around the same time, trying to find a match. And that is how my producer Henry and I met Amy.
<v Dan Taberski>目标是寻找嫌疑人身份的线索。他们只说名字，如果连名字都不说的话。所以我们寻找名字，或者可能是路牌，这样我们就能看到逮捕发生在哪里。然后我们会在同一时间左右的法庭记录中进行交叉核对，试图找到匹配项。这就是我的制作人 Henry 和我遇到 Amy 的经过。

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:28.100
<v Henry>What do you think? I'm going to just kind of mic it, like, back and forth.
<v Henry>你觉得呢？我只是想把它麦克风别在那儿，来回调整一下。

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:34.560
<v Amy>This is weird. It's like I'm being interrogated, but not by police officers. And remember it this time.
<v Amy>这很奇怪。感觉就像我在被审问，但不是警察。这次要记住。

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:41.640
<v Dan Taberski>Amy was on Live PD, season 2, episode 21. I'll play a little bit of that episode for you.
<v Dan Taberski>Amy 在《Live PD》第 2 季第 21 集里。我给你播一小段那一集。

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:43.610
<v Officer>Excuse me. Oh, is she drunk?
<v Officer>打扰一下。噢，她是喝醉了吗？

00:20:43.610 --> 00:20:51.700
<v Dan Taberski>A Spokane County Sheriff's deputy is responding to a call about a disturbance. And they find a woman in her late 30s. That's Amy.
<v Dan Taberski>斯波坎县警长的副手正在回应关于骚乱的报警。他们发现了一个 30 多岁的女人。那就是 Amy。

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:53.160
<v Officer>Then why are you sitting on the floor, crying?
<v Officer>那你为什么坐在地板上哭？

