00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:17.100
<v Dan Taberski>Question-- how does someone that drunk-- blackout drunk-- consent to being on Live PD, a live television show? I mean, it's hard enough for the show Cops to get them to sign, and that's taped months in advance.
<v Dan Taberski>问题来了——一个喝得那么醉——断片式醉酒的人——怎么能同意在《Live PD》这样一个直播电视节目上出现？我是说，连《Cops》节目都很难让他们签字，而且那还是提前几个月录制的。

00:24:17.100 --> 00:24:18.720
<v Dan Taberski>So did you consent?
<v Dan Taberski>所以你同意了吗？

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:20.910
<v Amy>No. Obviously not.
<v Amy>没有。显然没有。

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:24.050
<v Dan Taberski>Did they talk to you about the fact that you were on television?
<v Dan Taberski>他们跟你谈过你正在上电视的事吗？

00:24:24.050 --> 00:24:24.630
<v Amy>The police?
<v Amy>警察？

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:25.230
<v Dan Taberski>Yeah.
<v Dan Taberski>是的。

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:26.670
<v Amy>No.
<v Amy>没有。

00:24:26.670 --> 00:25:08.810
<v Dan Taberski>So this is the first thing we figure out. Whereas pretty much any other reality show needs the consent of people who appear on their show, Live PD makes a different case. Because it's live, or close to live-- they're actually on a delay. It's somewhere between 10 and 40 minutes. But because it's live, Live PD says, hey, we're basically news. And news doesn't need consent from suspects, so why should we? At least that's what they've argued in court. So for people like Amy, there is no consent involved here. You're on the show whether you like it or not. They don't blur her face, nothing.
<v Dan Taberski>所以这是我们弄清楚的第一件事。虽然几乎所有其他真人秀都需要出现的人同意，但《Live PD》的情况不同。因为它是在直播，或接近直播——实际上有延迟。大概在 10 到 40 分钟之间。但因为它是在直播，《Live PD》说，嘿，我们基本就是新闻。新闻不需要嫌疑人的同意，那我们为什么需要？至少他们在法庭上是这样辩称的。所以对于 Amy 这样的人来说，这里根本不涉及同意问题。不管你喜不喜欢，你都在节目上。他们没有模糊她的脸，什么都没有。

00:25:08.810 --> 00:25:27.560
<v Dan Taberski>Another thing we learned about Live PD and Cops, too, is that the police approve everything that goes on the air. With Live PD, that's where that 10- to 40-minute delay comes in. There's actually a hotline set up, like a Batphone, where the sheriffs can see what's about to go out on the air and just pick up the hotline and kill it.
<v Dan Taberski>关于《Live PD》和《Cops》，我们还学到的另一件事是，警方批准了所有播出的内容。《Live PD》的 10 到 40 分钟延迟就是为了这个。实际上有一条热线，就像红色电话一样，警长可以看到即将播出的内容，拿起热线直接切断它。

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:31.700
<v Henry>A lot of people watch Live PD. What do you imagine they're watching it for?
<v Henry>很多人看《Live PD》。你想象他们看这个是为了什么？

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:48.980
<v Amy>To watch fucking drunk-ass idiots make fools of themselves, myself-- sorry to be like that, but they are just fucking-- they have no problem belittling you, and humiliating you, and degrading you, just laughing at me and stuff in booking and just-- some of them calling me names.
<v Amy>看一群醉醺醺的傻瓜让自己出丑，我自己——抱歉这么说，但他们确实他妈的——他们毫无顾忌地贬低你、羞辱你、甚至在办案中心嘲笑我，叫我各种名字。

00:25:48.980 --> 00:25:50.270
<v Dan Taberski>What were they laughing at you for?
<v Dan Taberski>他们嘲笑你什么？

00:25:50.270 --> 00:25:54.530
<v Amy>Because I had made it on Live PD.
<v Amy>因为我上了《Live PD》。

00:25:54.530 --> 00:26:05.060
<v Dan Taberski>In fact, by the time Live PD was on again the next night, they had already edited footage of Amy into the opening of the show, a hot mix of the most salacious moments with Amy exclaiming--
<v Dan Taberski>事实上，等到第二天晚上《Live PD》再次播出时，他们已经把 Amy 的镜头剪辑进了开场，这成了最火爆的瞬间剪辑，Amy 大喊着——

