00:36:51.590 --> 00:37:10.220
<v Ira Glass>Dan Taberski. His podcast is called Running from Cops. It's made by Pineapple Street Media. The producers of Live PD did not respond to Dan and his team when they reached out. And we also reached out. We tried to do some fact-checking with them. They did not answer our fact check questions, but did issue a statement asserting the rights to broadcast what they do live.
<v Ira Glass>Dan Taberski。他的播客叫《Running from Cops》。由 Pineapple Street Media 制作。《Live PD》的制作人在 Dan 和他的团队联系时没有做出回应。我们也联系了。我们试图和他们进行了一些事实核查。他们没有回答我们的核查问题，但发表了一份声明，声称直播他们所做内容的权利。

00:37:10.220 --> 00:37:21.350
<v Ira Glass>Quote, "The First Amendment protects the right of the media to record people involved in public places in matters of public interest, which certainly includes the actions of law enforcement."
<v Ira Glass>引用：“第一修正案保护媒体记录公共利益事件中公共场所涉及人员的权利，其中当然包括执法部门的行为。”

00:37:21.350 --> 00:37:34.610
<v Ira Glass>Coming up on TV, but there is no way you could ever know it, because you don't know what TV is. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来在电视上，但你永远无法知道，因为你不知道电视是什么。那是来自芝加哥公共广播电台，一分钟后我们的节目继续。

00:37:34.610 --> 00:37:51.220
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, I'm on TV. Stories of people who are used to being on the watching side of television, the normal side of television, who suddenly, without choosing, are placed on the other side of the screen. Or maybe the best way to say that is they're inside the screen. I don't know.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是 Ira Glass。今天的节目，“我在电视上”。讲述那些习惯于站在电视旁、电视正常那一侧的人，突然在没有选择的情况下被置于屏幕另一侧的故事。或者说最好的说法是他们被置于屏幕之内。我不知道。

00:37:51.220 --> 00:38:18.830
<v Ira Glass>What's that do to you? We've arrived at Act 2 of our program. Act 2, "Born To Play The Part." I know somebody who whenever we would watch a film or TV show, if there was a dog in the scene, they always did the same thing-- they would turn to me and say, dog doesn't know it's in a movie. Or dog doesn't know that's Mel Gibson. Dog doesn't know that Neil Patrick Harris. Same thing if a baby showed up. Baby doesn't know that's Meryl Streep. Baby doesn't know it's in a film.
<v Ira Glass>那对你有什么影响？我们进入了节目的第二幕。第二幕，“生来就扮演这个角色”。我认识一个人，每当我们看电影或电视节目时，如果场景里有一只狗，他们总是做同样的事——他们会转向我说，狗不知道它在电影里。或者狗不知道那是 Mel Gibson。狗不知道那是 Neil Patrick Harris。如果出现个婴儿也是一样。婴儿不知道那是 Meryl Streep。婴儿不知道它在电影里。

00:38:18.830 --> 00:38:29.840
<v Ira Glass>One of our producers, Bim Adewunmi, is also a big noticer or of babies on television, including one in particular that she's become slightly obsessed with. Here's Bim.
<v Ira Glass>我们的制作人之一 Bim Adewunmi 也是个大婴儿关注者，包括她变得有点着迷的一个特定婴儿。这是 Bim。

00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:49.170
<v Bim Adewunmi>OK. So it's an uncharacteristically peaceful scene in the sixth season of The Walking Dead. No zombies, no gore. There's a man, the hero of the show, Rick Grimes and his teenage son Carl. The whole scene has a cloud of awkward family tension, but nothing more perilous than that.
<v Bim Adewunmi>好吧。所以这是《行尸走肉》第六季中一个罕见的平静场景。没有僵尸，没有血腥。有一个人，节目的英雄 Rick Grimes 和他十几岁的儿子 Carl。整个场景有一层尴尬的家庭紧张感，但没有比这更危险的了。

00:38:49.170 --> 00:38:54.300
<v Rick Grimes>Get your stuff. Gabriel can take care of Judith while we're gone.
<v Rick Grimes>拿好你的东西。我们离开时 Gabriel 可以照顾 Judith。

00:38:54.300 --> 00:38:56.220
<v Carl Grimes>I'm not coming.
<v Carl Grimes>我不去。

00:38:56.220 --> 00:39:21.680
<v Bim Adewunmi>But wait. There's a third character in the scene. She's wearing a lilac smock dress, and she's got a pacifier in her mouth. She's a baby, and she's adorable. Her name is Judith. So anyway, Rick is carrying Judith towards Carl in the street. And as they approach, the baby spots the teen and she waves. It's obviously unscripted, because how could you script a toddler?
<v Bim Adewunmi>但等等。场景中还有第三个角色。她穿着一件丁香色的罩衫裙，嘴里叼着个安抚奶嘴。她是个婴儿，她很可爱。她的名字叫 Judith。总之，Rick 正抱着 Judith 在街上向 Carl 走去。当他们靠近时，婴儿发现了这个少年并挥手。这显然不是写在剧本里的，因为你怎么能给蹒跚学步的孩子写剧本呢？

00:39:21.680 --> 00:39:47.120
<v Bim Adewunmi>And you can see the actor playing her dad, Andrew Lincoln, look down and smile in surprise. All through the scene Judy goes through baby's greatest hits. She fidgets. She sucks on her pacifier. She makes sounds that the subtitles caption as babbling. And with one arm wrapped around Rick's bicep, she swivels her head back and forth, just observing her environment.
<v Bim Adewunmi>你可以看到饰演她父亲的演员 Andrew Lincoln 低头惊讶地微笑。在整个场景中，Judy 经历了婴儿最精彩的瞬间。她坐立不安。她吮吸着安抚奶嘴。她发出字幕标注为“咿呀学语”的声音。一只手臂环绕着 Rick 的二头肌，她来回旋转着头，只是观察着她的环境。

00:39:47.120 --> 00:40:12.270
<v Bim Adewunmi>And then she does the one thing you're not supposed to do. She spots the camera, and then proceeds very happily to break the fourth wall. She stares, and I mean stares down the barrel of the camera. Her focus, laser-sharp. Above her head, acting with a capital A, is happening. This is the highest-rated cable show in America.
<v Bim Adewunmi>然后她做了你绝对不该做的一件事。她发现了摄像机，然后很开心地打破了第四面墙。她盯着，我是说盯着摄像机的镜头。她的焦点，激光般锐利。在她头顶上，正在发生着大写的“演戏”。这是美国收视率最高的有线电视节目。

00:40:12.270 --> 00:40:29.870
<v Bim Adewunmi>But to this baby, who knows nothing about craft, or dramaturgy, or anything else or the televisual arts, there's only a great big camera. And as far as she understands life, it's there to be looked at. A few things go through my mind whenever I watch this scene.
<v Bim Adewunmi>但对这个不知道手艺、戏剧学或其他任何电视艺术的婴儿来说，只有一台巨大的摄像机。就她对生活的理解而言，它是用来被观看的。每当我看到这个场景时，会有一些想法闪过脑海。

00:40:29.870 --> 00:40:55.840
<v Bim Adewunmi>That this must have been the best take of the day. I don't know why, but that tickles me. The idea of a Hollywood set being at the mercy of a baby's whims is just joyous to me. Judith has been around on the show since the third season, the first baby born to our heroes since the dead began roaming the Earth. The parade of babies who played her in seasons 3 to 5 were fine.
<v Bim Adewunmi>这一定是当天拍得最好的一条。我不知道为什么，但这让我很高兴。好莱坞片场受婴儿心血来潮摆布的想法对我来说真是太快乐了。Judith 从第三季开始就在节目中出现了，这是自从死者开始在地球上游荡以来，我们英雄中出生的第一个婴儿。在第三季到第五季中饰演她的婴儿阵列都很好。

00:40:55.840 --> 00:41:17.440
<v Bim Adewunmi>But the Judith they cast in season 6, ah, the platonic ideal of cute baby. Cheeks, heavy and jowly. Mouth rosebud. Eyes wide and bright. Can a baby have charisma? Well, this baby had charisma. First of all, she was a surprisingly good actor sometimes.
<v Bim Adewunmi>但他们在第六季选角的 Judith，啊，可爱婴儿的柏拉图式理想。脸颊，沉重而丰满。嘴唇如玫瑰花蕾。眼睛又大又亮。婴儿能有魅力吗？嗯，这个婴儿有魅力。首先，她有时是个出人意料的好演员。

00:41:17.440 --> 00:41:20.630
<v Bim Adewunmi>Take season 6, episode 9, "No Way Out."
<v Bim Adewunmi>看第六季第 9 集，“没有出路”。

00:41:20.630 --> 00:41:23.910
<v Rick Grimes>All right. New plan.
<v Rick Grimes>好的。新计划。

00:41:23.910 --> 00:41:39.720
<v Bim Adewunmi>Judith, along with her parents and brother, are trying to escape a herd of walkers who have broken into their community. The thing is, walkers can smell humans. And so the survivors have to mask their scent by wearing sheets smeared in Walker guts to get through the horde.
<v Bim Adewunmi>Judith 和她的父母及兄弟正试图逃离一群闯入他们社区的行尸。问题是，行尸能闻到人类的味道。所以幸存者必须通过穿上涂抹了行尸内脏的床单来遮盖他们的气味，以穿过那群行尸。

00:41:39.720 --> 00:41:47.740
<v Bim Adewunmi>Eventually, the group is forced to split up, and Judy gets passed from her brother, who's carrying her under his sheet, to another member of the group.
<v Bim Adewunmi>最终，小组被迫分开，Judy 被从抱着她的兄弟那里转移给小组的另一个成员。

00:41:47.740 --> 00:41:49.680
<v Carl Grimes>I'll take her.
<v Carl Grimes>我来带她。

00:41:49.680 --> 00:42:23.520
<v Bim Adewunmi>There's a tight shot of Judy's little face as she gets transferred, fake blood on her cheek. And she looks 100% stressed out, like she's thinking, for god's sake, what now? And of course she thinks that. Look around her. It's all zombies. She's not acting at all, which of course makes her the best actor in the scene. Talk about method. We get Judith in short bursts, mere seconds at a time. I've inadvertently watched her grow up. In the first episode of season 9, she talks.
<v Bim Adewunmi>当她被转移时，有一个 Judy 小脸的特写镜头，脸上带着假血。她看起来 100% 压力巨大，好像在想，看在上帝的份上，现在又怎么了？当然她会那样想。看看她周围。到处都是僵尸。她根本没有在演戏，这当然使她成为场景中最好的演员。谈谈方法派表演。我们只在短时间内得到 Judith 的镜头，每次仅几秒钟。我不经意间看着她长大。在第九季的第一集中，她会说话了。

00:42:23.520 --> 00:42:26.270
<v Bim Adewunmi>[CHILD BABBLING]
<v Bim Adewunmi>[婴儿咿呀学语]

00:42:26.270 --> 00:42:43.980
<v Bim Adewunmi>Judith is actually played by two babies. That tends to be the case because of pesky things like labor laws and child welfare. You can swap out one identical twin for another without messing up continuity. The twins that play Judith, their names are Sofia and Chloe Garcia-Frizzi.
<v Bim Adewunmi>Judith 实际上是由两个婴儿扮演的。因为像劳动法和儿童福利这种讨厌的东西，情况往往如此。你可以替换一个一模一样的双胞胎而不破坏连续性。饰演 Judith 的双胞胎名字叫 Sofia 和 Chloe Garcia-Frizzi。

00:42:43.980 --> 00:43:02.810
<v Bim Adewunmi>I cannot stress this enough. The Garcia-Frizzi twins might be the cutest babies I have ever seen, on telly or in real life. Top three, definitely. They arrived in their butterball prime, all round and squishy. No hard edges to them. And now they are five years old.
<v Bim Adewunmi>我怎么强调都不为过。Garcia-Frizzi 双胞胎可能是我在电视上或现实生活中见过最可爱的婴儿。前三名，绝对的。她们正处于婴儿肥的鼎盛期，圆润柔软。身上没有坚硬的边缘。现在她们五岁了。

00:43:02.810 --> 00:43:08.600
<v Bim Adewunmi>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Bim Adewunmi>[音乐播放]

00:43:08.600 --> 00:43:27.390
<v Bim Adewunmi>It's funny to think of babies as famous, as being recognized by fans in the streets. Because being a baby, you're often the center of attention. You have someone who prepares your food and drives you around. It's not so different from being famous. There's no way a baby would know they were getting special attention.
<v Bim Adewunmi>把婴儿当成名人，在街上被粉丝认出来是很有趣的。因为作为婴儿，你往往是关注的焦点。你有准备食物和开车送你的人。这和出名没什么两样。婴儿不可能知道他们受到了特别关注。

00:43:27.390 --> 00:43:36.820
<v Bim Adewunmi>Babies just expect that. I talked to the Garcia-Frizzi twins' mother, Tiffany, to ask if the girls knew they were famous now that they're five.
<v Bim Adewunmi>婴儿只是期待那样。我和 Garcia-Frizzi 双胞胎的母亲 Tiffany 谈过，问孩子们在五岁时是否知道她们出名了。

00:43:36.820 --> 00:43:51.710
<v Tiffany Garcia-frizzi>It's actually really, really funny, because until my oldest daughter told them that they were famous, they legitimately did not now. Like they would go up to kids at the park, and they'd ask them what TV show they're on. And it's like, no not everybody's on a TV show.
<v Tiffany Garcia-frizzi>其实真的很有趣，因为直到我大女儿告诉她们她们很有名之前，她们真的不知道。就像她们在公园里走向孩子们，问他们上什么电视节目。就像是，不，不是每个人都上电视节目。

00:43:51.710 --> 00:43:58.990
<v Tiffany Garcia-frizzi>So until their older sister was like no, you guys are on TV. You're famous. They're like, what's famous? I was like, it means nothing. It means nothing.
<v Tiffany Garcia-frizzi>所以直到她们的大姐说“不，你们上电视了。你们出名了”。她们问“出名是什么？”我说，这毫无意义。这毫无意义。

00:43:58.990 --> 00:44:06.640
<v Bim Adewunmi>They get recognized from time to time, like when they went trick or treating and the people at the house were dressed like characters from "The Walking Dead."
<v Bim Adewunmi>她们时不时会被认出来，比如她们去“不给糖就捣蛋”时，房子里的人打扮得像《行尸走肉》里的角色。

00:44:06.640 --> 00:44:21.250
<v Tiffany Garcia-frizzi>I think they're starting to come aware of, like, that other people do know who they are that aren't necessarily people we know, if that makes sense. Like, strangers know who they are. I don't think they really necessarily connected it to fame yet.
<v Tiffany Garcia-frizzi>我想她们开始意识到，有些人知道她们是谁，但不一定是我们认识的人，如果这说得通的话。就像，陌生人知道她们是谁。我不认为她们真正地把这和名气联系起来。

00:44:21.250 --> 00:44:29.250
<v Tiffany Garcia-frizzi>But they will tell people they're Judith Grimes. Like, they definitely know that they play pretend on TV. But they don't know it's acting. They just think it's pretend.
<v Tiffany Garcia-frizzi>但她们会告诉别人她们是 Judith Grimes。就像，她们绝对知道她们在电视上玩角色扮演。但她们不知道那是在演戏。她们只是觉得那是假装。

00:44:29.250 --> 00:44:52.920
<v Bim Adewunmi>Fame changes us, takes us further away from what we once were, which is to say innocent and pure. Famous babies don't know they're famous, so they're immune to its dangers. When you think about it, babies are the only beings who should be famous.
<v Bim Adewunmi>名气改变了我们，把我们带离了曾经的我们，也就是天真和纯洁。出名的婴儿不知道他们出名了，所以他们对名气的危险免疫。当你仔细想想，婴儿是唯一应该出名的存在。

00:44:52.920 --> 00:45:06.390
<v Ira Glass>Bim Adewunmi is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>Bim Adewunmi 是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:45:06.390 --> 00:45:22.530
<v Ira Glass>Act 3, "Lina with an N." So we now turn to two girls. One of them has this big moment in her life end up on national television, and she had no choice about that. The other sees that moment on television, and it sticks with her for years.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“Lina，拼写是 N”。所以我们现在转向两个女孩。其中一个在她生命中这个巨大时刻登上了全国电视，她对此别无选择。另一个在电视上看到了那一刻，并且在几年里一直萦绕在心头。

00:45:22.530 --> 00:45:37.650
<v Ira Glass>The second girl in the story is Lina Misitzis, one of the producers of our show. A piece of background you need to know to understand this story-- she was really into musical theater as a kid. Listened to musicals, fantasized about being in them, which is why this story stayed with her. Here she is.
<v Ira Glass>故事里的第二个女孩是 Lina Misitzis，我们节目的制作人之一。你需要了解一点背景才能理解这个故事——她小时候非常迷恋音乐剧。听音乐剧，幻想身处其中，这就是为什么这个故事一直萦绕在她心头。在这里。

00:45:37.650 --> 00:45:59.010
<v Lina Misitzis>I know when it started. It was in Greece-- the country, not the musical. I spent part of my childhood there. I was six or seven years old when I found a two-disk CD set at the bottom of some sales bin, a best of Broadway compilation from various European tours of Broadway shows, all in different languages. I remember a German "I Am What I Am" from "La Cage aux Folles."
<v Lina Misitzis>我知道它是从什么时候开始的。是在希腊——那个国家，不是那部音乐剧。我童年的一部分在那里度过。我六七岁时，在某个特价箱底部找到了一套双碟 CD，是一套来自不同百老汇演出欧洲巡演的百老汇精选集，都是不同语言的。我记得《La Cage aux Folles》（一笼傻鸟）里德语版的“I Am What I Am”（我就是我）。

00:45:59.010 --> 00:45:59.970
<v Lina Misitzis>[MUSIC - "I AM WHAT I AM']
<v Lina Misitzis>[音乐 - “I AM WHAT I AM”]

00:45:59.970 --> 00:46:04.300
<v Lina Misitzis>[NON-ENGLISH SINGING]
<v Lina Misitzis>[非英语演唱]

00:46:04.300 --> 00:46:09.920
<v Ira Glass>I listened over and over, arms outstretched, pretending I was onstage, spotlit.
<v Ira Glass>我一遍又一遍地听，双臂伸展，假装我在舞台上，处于聚光灯下。

00:46:09.920 --> 00:46:22.280
<v Ira Glass>[NON-ENGLISH SINGING]
<v Ira Glass>[非英语演唱]

00:46:22.280 --> 00:46:46.130
<v Ira Glass>It only escalated from there. Back in America, in my bedroom in Virginia, I Scotch-taped up pictures of Ethel Merman, Bernadette Peters, Bebe Neuwirth. On weekends, I checked casting calls in the Washington Post. My mom's rule was if an audition was less than five miles away, I could try out. I recorded the Tonys on VHS each year, watched it over and over.
<v Ira Glass>此后一发不可收拾。回到美国，在我弗吉尼亚的卧室里，我用透明胶带贴上了 Ethel Merman、Bernadette Peters、Bebe Neuwirth 的照片。周末，我会查看《华盛顿邮报》上的选角通告。我妈妈的规矩是，如果试镜地点在五英里之内，我就可以尝试。我每年都用录像带录下托尼奖，一遍又一遍地看。

00:46:46.130 --> 00:47:14.880
<v Ira Glass>Same for "Sound Of Music" and "Fiddler on the Roof." But the tape I watched the most, the one that spoke to me more than any other, was this 1997 TV special. Barbara Walters hosted, and it was called "Broadway's New Annie: Search For A Star." and if the title doesn't give it away, here's the premise. The musical "Annie" was coming back to Broadway and the show's director needed to find a star. And this was how he decided to do it, on TV.
<v Ira Glass>《音乐之声》和《屋顶上的小提琴手》也一样。但我看的最多的录像带，对我说话最多的，是 1997 年的这个电视特辑。Barbara Walters 主持的，叫《百老汇的新安妮：寻找明星》。如果标题没说明一切，前提就是这样。音乐剧《安妮》要重返百老汇，导演需要找一个明星。这就是他决定做的方式，在电视上。

00:47:14.880 --> 00:47:21.910
<v Woman>Little girls, little girls, 2,000 little girls all sharing the same dream.
<v Woman>小女孩，小女孩，2000 个小女孩都怀着同一个梦想。

00:47:21.910 --> 00:47:24.060
<v Girl>Annie, Annie, Annie, Annie, Annie.
<v Girl>安妮，安妮，安妮，安妮，安妮。

00:47:24.060 --> 00:47:43.530
<v Lina Misitzis>I was 8, the same age as those girls. I could have auditioned if I'd known about it. It was a nationwide contest sponsored by the department store Macy's, and there was a Macy's less than five miles from my house. To watch, I dressed up like an orphan, and the closest thing I came up with was wearing my older brother's underwear.
<v Lina Misitzis>我当时 8 岁，和那些女孩一样大。如果我知道的话我也可以去试镜。这是一个由梅西百货赞助的全国性竞赛，离我家不到五英里就有一家梅西百货。为了观看，我装扮成孤儿，我能想到的最接近的东西是穿上我哥哥的内衣。

00:47:43.530 --> 00:48:13.500
<v Lina Misitzis>I'd stand in front of the TV transfixed, so close my mom would yell at me that I was going to damage my eyes. And the person I was watching for was this one girl named Harley who reminded me of me because she was special, perfect for the role in every way, which I figured I'd be. Although Harley had the advantage of also looking the part-- long red wavy hair, chubby cheeks. The sort of aw, shucks grin you want to see on Annie's face. Except.
<v Lina Misitzis>我会站在电视机前出神，离得那么近，我妈妈会对我大喊我会弄坏眼睛。我一直在观察的那个人是一个叫 Harley 的女孩，她让我想起我自己，因为她很特别，在各方面都很适合这个角色，我想我会是那样的。尽管 Harley 还有看起来很像这个角色的优势——长长的红色波浪卷发，肉嘟嘟的脸颊。那种你想在安妮脸上看到的“啊，哎呀”的咧嘴笑。除了。

00:48:13.500 --> 00:48:15.640
<v Barbara Walters>But then, it was Harley's turn to sing.
<v Barbara Walters>但后来，轮到 Harley 唱歌了。

00:48:15.640 --> 00:48:16.800
<v Harley Ott>(SINGING) Tomorrow.
<v Harley Ott>(演唱) 明天。

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:24.590
<v Martin Charnin>I don't want you to do that. You're going tomorrow, tomorrow. Tomorrow! Too! Loud! (SINGING) Too.
<v Martin Charnin>我不想让你那样做。你要唱明天，明天。明天！太！响了！(演唱) 太。

00:48:24.590 --> 00:48:25.070
<v Harley Ott>(SINGING) Too.
<v Harley Ott>(演唱) 太。

00:48:25.070 --> 00:48:33.250
<v Martin Charnin>Too. Too. Too! Too!
<v Martin Charnin>太。太。太！太！

00:48:33.250 --> 00:48:40.800
<v Harley Ott>Just sometimes, I get kind of scared, and it was really scary. So it kind of like came out.
<v Harley Ott>有时候，我感到有点害怕，而且真的很吓人。所以它就像那样出来了。

00:48:40.800 --> 00:48:58.650
<v Lina Misitzis>She's scared, which is kind of understandable. The guy she's auditioning for is Martin Charnin. He wrote "Annie" back in 1976. He was the show's original director. That's him you hear singing along with her. Martin takes Harley by the hand, and leads her away from all the other girls.
<v Lina Misitzis>她很害怕，这是可以理解的。她试镜的对象是 Martin Charnin。他在 1976 年写了《安妮》。他是该剧最初的导演。你听到和他一起唱歌的就是他。Martin 牵着 Harley 的手，把她带离了其他女孩。

00:48:58.650 --> 00:49:06.920
<v Lina Misitzis>He cups her chin in his palm, and places her on the floor by his feet, and together they watch more Annie hopefuls audition.
<v Lina Misitzis>他用手心捧着她的下巴，把她放在他脚边的地板上，他们一起观看更多安妮候选人的试镜。

00:49:06.920 --> 00:49:10.810
<v Harley Ott>Then I sat with the director, and then he let me watch.
<v Harley Ott>然后我和导演坐在一起，他让我看。

00:49:10.810 --> 00:49:13.020
<v Martin Charnin>So 149 was singing with you, right?
<v Martin Charnin>所以 149 号在和你一起唱歌，对吧？

00:49:13.020 --> 00:49:18.980
<v Barbara Walters>Something about the little girl's face makes Martin want to see her again. She makes the finals.
<v Barbara Walters>那个小女孩的脸让 Martin 想再见她一次。她进入了决赛。

00:49:18.980 --> 00:49:24.360
<v Harley Ott>I'm just going to think about just smile, and do your best.
<v Harley Ott>我只想想想微笑，尽力而为。

00:49:24.360 --> 00:49:32.490
<v Lina Misitzis>I loved this story because Harley just needed to be noticed by the right person, just like I needed.
<v Lina Misitzis>我喜欢这个故事，因为 Harley 只需要被对的人注意到，就像我也需要的那样。

00:49:32.490 --> 00:49:51.080
<v Barbara Walters>August 7, at a nondescript rehearsal hall in Midtown Manhattan, the finalists have gathered selected from 2,000 of the last auditions. Harley Ott, one of Martin's favorites, is back from Jersey. She has another chance to sing, but again is struck with stage fright.
<v Barbara Walters>8 月 7 日，在曼哈顿中城一个不起眼的排练厅里，决赛选手汇集在一起，是从 2000 个最后试镜中挑选出来的。Martin 的最爱之一 Harley Ott 从泽西岛回来了。她有另一次唱歌的机会，但再次被舞台恐惧感袭击。

00:49:51.080 --> 00:49:55.010
<v Harley Ott>(SINGING) You're always a day [INAUDIBLE].
<v Harley Ott>(演唱) 你永远是一天 [听不清]。

00:49:55.010 --> 00:49:55.500
<v Harley Ott>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Harley Ott>[音乐播放]

00:49:55.500 --> 00:49:57.460
<v Martin Charnin>Day a-
<v Martin Charnin>一天——

00:49:57.460 --> 00:49:59.440
<v Harley Ott>A-
<v Harley Ott>啊——

00:49:59.440 --> 00:50:04.090
<v Lina Misitzis>It doesn't go well, and still, later that day she gets a third chance.
<v Lina Misitzis>进行得不顺利，尽管如此，那天晚些时候她得到了第三次机会。

00:50:04.090 --> 00:50:10.420
<v Barbara Walters>Martin, who thinks she might be right for the role of an orphan, still believes he could overcome her nerves in rehearsal.
<v Barbara Walters>Martin 认为她可能适合演孤儿这个角色，仍然相信他能在排练中克服她的紧张。

00:50:10.420 --> 00:50:32.520
<v Lina Misitzis>OK, so now she's out of the running for Annie. But she may become an orphan on Broadway-- the kind of thing I'd have died for. As far as I was concerned, Harley had made it. But then comes the moment I haven't been able to understand for more than 20 years. As a kid I found it upsetting, as an adult baffling. Martin walks into the hall to grab Harley.
<v Lina Misitzis>好吧，现在她失去了演安妮的机会。但她可能会成为百老汇的一个孤儿——那种我会为之去死的事情。就我而言，Harley 成功了。但随后发生了我 20 多年来一直无法理解的时刻。小时候我觉得它令人不安，成年后觉得它令人困惑。Martin 走进大厅去抓 Harley。

00:50:32.520 --> 00:50:34.600
<v Martin Charnin>Is Harley here?
<v Martin Charnin>Harley 在这儿吗？

00:50:34.600 --> 00:50:37.250
<v Barbara Walters>But when he goes to find her parents.
<v Barbara Walters>但他去找她父母时。

00:50:37.250 --> 00:50:39.220
<v Martin Charnin>Brian, where did Harley's parents go?
<v Martin Charnin>Brian，Harley 的父母去哪儿了？

00:50:39.220 --> 00:50:43.510
<v Man>I had them right in that chair, right here all three of them.
<v Man>我刚才让他们坐在那把椅子上，就在这里，他们三个人。

00:50:43.510 --> 00:50:45.370
<v Man>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Man>[干扰声音]

00:50:45.370 --> 00:50:47.070
<v Barbara Walters>He finds they've left.
<v Barbara Walters>他发现他们离开了。

00:50:47.070 --> 00:51:02.080
<v Lina Misitzis>On screen, you see a confused Martin looking around anxiously. He's still not done with her, still picturing her in his cast list, but she was gone. For a moment, he stands there probably thinking exactly what I'm thinking.
<v Lina Misitzis>屏幕上，你可以看到困惑的 Martin 焦虑地环顾四周。他还没搞定她，还在剧组名单里想象她，但她不见了。有一刻，他站在那里，可能在想和我完全一样的事情。

00:51:02.080 --> 00:51:21.500
<v Lina Misitzis>Why the hell would you ever walk out on that? Harley threw away her shot, and ever since I've wondered why. So I tracked her down. She was really surprised to hear from me. It was so long ago, she said, but she was also totally game to talk. Harley and I actually have a lot in common.
<v Lina Misitzis>为什么你他妈会放弃那个机会？Harley 丢掉了她的机会，从那以后我一直在想为什么。所以我追踪到了她。她真的对我的联系感到惊讶。那是很久以前的事了，她说，但她也完全乐意谈谈。Harley 和我其实有很多共同点。

00:51:21.500 --> 00:51:29.110
<v Lina Misitzis>We're both from the East Coast, we're both 30 years old, born just 10 days apart. She remembers everything about the audition.
<v Lina Misitzis>我们都来自东海岸，我们都 30 岁，出生日期仅差 10 天。她记得关于试镜的一切。

00:51:29.110 --> 00:51:40.270
<v Harley Ott>And then I remember these, like, boards we had to walk under. It was like you had to be under a certain height to even go into the audition, cause you had to walk under these kids-only things.
<v Harley Ott>然后我记得这些，就像我们必须走过去的板子。就像为了去试镜你必须低于一定身高，因为你必须走过这些小孩专用的东西。

00:51:40.270 --> 00:51:41.890
<v Lina Misitzis>They decided that orphans were a certain height?
<v Lina Misitzis>他们决定孤儿一定要达到一定身高？

00:51:41.890 --> 00:51:48.520
<v Harley Ott>I guess so. I mean, I was certainly getting under that. So I'd probably still get under that today.
<v Harley Ott>我想是这样。我是说，我肯定低于那个。所以我今天可能还是能通过。

00:51:48.520 --> 00:52:09.400
<v Lina Misitzis>By the way, Harley has dwarfism. That's what she meant by that joke. She's 4 foot 6. I pretty quickly got to the point. Why did you walk out that day? And the answer was not what I expected at all. I thought Harley was bombing because of what Barbara Walters said, because she had stage fright. But it wasn't nerves stopping her.
<v Lina Misitzis>顺便说一句，Harley 有侏儒症。那是她那个笑话的意思。她身高 4 英尺 6 英寸。我很快进入了正题。你那天为什么要走掉？答案完全出乎我的意料。我以为 Harley 是因为 Barbara Walters 说的那样炸掉了，因为她有舞台恐惧症。但不是紧张阻止了她。

00:52:09.400 --> 00:52:17.530
<v Harley Ott>That's not stage fright. That's frustration that I can't sing. I'm not scared, I'm pissed. Like, I'm mad at myself in that moment.
<v Harley Ott>那不是舞台恐惧症。那是由于我唱不了歌而产生的挫败感。我不怕，我生气。就像，那一刻我在生我自己的气。

00:52:17.530 --> 00:52:20.330
<v Harley Ott>(SINGING) You're always a day--
<v Harley Ott>(演唱) 你永远是一天——

00:52:20.330 --> 00:52:21.260
<v Martin Charnin>A-
<v Martin Charnin>啊——

00:52:21.260 --> 00:52:22.670
<v Harley Ott>A-
<v Harley Ott>啊——

00:52:22.670 --> 00:52:42.090
<v Harley Ott>I have no pitch, and the poor man was trying so hard to get me to sing the right note, and I just couldn't do it. Start to get hot. Your hands start to get clammy, your heart starts to race. All these people are now looking at me. And I don't know what to do.
<v Harley Ott>我完全不在调上，那个可怜的人努力想让我唱对音符，但我就是唱不出来。开始感到热了。你的手开始出汗，你的心开始跳动。所有这些人现在都在看着我。我不知道该做什么。

00:52:42.090 --> 00:52:46.160
<v Lina Misitzis>Which of course casts her sudden disappearance in an entirely different light.
<v Lina Misitzis>这当然以完全不同的角度展现了她突然的失踪。

00:52:46.160 --> 00:53:19.580
<v Harley Ott>It was not a feeling of fear of being on stage, or fear of auditioning. It was just an overwhelming feeling of this not being right for me. Think it was just this isn't what I want to be doing right now. It stopped being fun, and I just looked at both of my parents and I said, I'm done. I don't-- I don't want to do this anymore. I'm ready to go. And there was absolutely no questioning of me on that at all. They said, OK, let's go.
<v Harley Ott>那不是对舞台的恐惧，也不是对试镜的恐惧。那只是一种 overwhelming 的感觉，觉得这不适合我。我想这只是我现在不想做的事情。它不再有趣了，我只是看着我的父母说，我做完了。我不要——我不想再做这个了。我准备走了。对此绝对没有任何疑问。他们说，好的，我们走吧。

00:53:19.580 --> 00:53:27.260
<v Lina Misitzis>Were you embarrassed leaving? Did you say goodbye to people? Like, the way it's framed in the show is this very abrupt thing. But how did it feel in the moment? What did it look like?
<v Lina Misitzis>你离开时尴尬吗？你和人们道别了吗？节目里的框架是这非常突然的一件事。但在那一刻感觉如何？它看起来像什么？

00:53:27.260 --> 00:53:35.100
<v Harley Ott>The moment my parents said we could leave, I was comfortable. I was like all right, guys. Let's get out of here. Let's go get a snack.
<v Harley Ott>我父母说我们可以离开的那一刻，我很舒服。我心想，好吧，伙计们。让我们离开这里吧。去吃点东西。

00:53:35.100 --> 00:53:41.030
<v Lina Misitzis>Why she didn't stick around that day, it wasn't an act of fear. It was an act of courage.
<v Lina Misitzis>她那天没待在那里，这不是恐惧的行为。这是一种勇气的行为。

00:53:41.030 --> 00:53:54.440
<v Harley Ott>I feel like I've always been pretty in tune with myself. And I know when something is not right. And I've done this with other things in my life, where I've gotten pretty far with something, and then I'm like eh, I think we're done.
<v Harley Ott>我觉得我一直很了解自己。我知道什么时候事情不对劲。我生活中也对其他事情做过同样的事，我在这方面走得很远，然后我想，呃，我想我们做完了。

00:53:54.440 --> 00:53:58.390
<v Lina Misitzis>So there's this like other little person inside of you.
<v Lina Misitzis>所以你心里有另一个小人儿。

00:53:58.390 --> 00:53:59.270
<v Harley Ott>Is that a short joke?
<v Harley Ott>那是身高歧视笑话吗？

00:53:59.270 --> 00:54:13.510
<v Lina Misitzis>It wasn't a short joke, but when I said it, I was worried that you might think it is. But it wasn't. So there was, I'm going to start over. So there was a voice in your head speaking to you in a language that you understand, but couldn't quite speak yet.
<v Lina Misitzis>这不是身高歧视笑话，但当我说了之后，我担心你可能会那么想。但这并不是。所以有，我要重新开始。所以你脑子里有一个声音用一种你理解但还说不出的语言在和你说话。

00:54:13.510 --> 00:54:15.410
<v Harley Ott>100%, yes.
<v Harley Ott>100%，是的。

00:54:15.410 --> 00:54:18.530
<v Lina Misitzis>But what you were able to interpret in the moment was?
<v Lina Misitzis>但你在那一刻能解读的是？

00:54:18.530 --> 00:54:20.270
<v Harley Ott>This isn't right for me.
<v Harley Ott>这不适合我。

00:54:20.270 --> 00:54:21.250
<v Lina Misitzis>And I don't feel good.
<v Lina Misitzis>而且我感觉不好。

00:54:21.250 --> 00:54:22.120
<v Harley Ott>Yeah.
<v Harley Ott>是的。

00:54:22.120 --> 00:54:23.100
<v Lina Misitzis>And I need to get out.
<v Lina Misitzis>而且我需要离开。

00:54:23.100 --> 00:54:24.680
<v Harley Ott>I need to leave.
<v Harley Ott>我需要离开。

00:54:24.680 --> 00:54:38.520
<v Lina Misitzis>So at 7, Harley understood enough to know she wasn't good enough. She walked away. She felt fine. Me, it took me much longer to figure it out. I auditioned for the 8th grade musical, and didn't get in.
<v Lina Misitzis>所以 7 岁时，Harley 理解自己不够好。她走开了。她感觉很好。对我来说，我花了很多时间才弄明白。我试镜了 8 年级的音乐剧，没进。

00:54:38.520 --> 00:54:58.730
<v Lina Misitzis>I auditioned for the 9th grade musical, didn't get in. I auditioned for the 10th grade play, and didn't get in. I tried transferring to Interlochen, an elite arts boarding school for teens. Didn't get in. My senior year of high school, all the kids enrolled in theater were given one song to sing in an end-of-year revue.
<v Lina Misitzis>我试镜了 9 年级的音乐剧，没进。我试镜了 10 年级的戏剧，没进。我尝试转到 Interlochen，一所为青少年开设的精英艺术寄宿学校。没进。高四那年，所有选修戏剧的孩子都在年终回顾演出中获得了一首歌唱的机会。

00:54:58.730 --> 00:55:03.480
<v Lina Misitzis>The teacher gave me an easy one, a song from the musical "Hairspray." About growing up.
<v Lina Misitzis>老师给了我一首容易的，来自音乐剧《发胶星梦》的歌。关于长大的。

00:55:03.480 --> 00:55:04.450
<v Lina Misitzis>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Lina Misitzis>[音乐播放]

00:55:04.450 --> 00:55:10.230
<v Lina Misitzis>(SINGING) Once upon a time, I used to play with Kens. But now that I'm a woman, I like bigger men.
<v Lina Misitzis>(演唱) 曾经，我用来玩肯尼娃娃。但现在我是一个女人了，我喜欢更大的男人。

00:55:10.230 --> 00:55:18.250
<v Lina Misitzis>When the song shifted into the high notes, I talked the lyrics instead of singing them, hoping it'd make it look intentional, like a character choice.
<v Lina Misitzis>当歌曲进入高音时，我用说话代替了唱歌，希望这看起来是故意的，像一种角色选择。

00:55:18.250 --> 00:55:19.220
<v Lina Misitzis>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Lina Misitzis>[音乐播放]

00:55:19.220 --> 00:55:23.590
<v Lina Misitzis>(SINGING) [INAUDIBLE]
<v Lina Misitzis>(演唱) [听不清]

00:55:23.590 --> 00:55:41.400
<v Lina Misitzis>Harley's a bad singer, but she's obviously got something. She's a good actor. What you don't find out about her in that Barbara Walters special is that she actually went onto a pretty successful child acting career. She starred in a Nick at Nite short, multiple commercials, even a Val Kilmer movie. But me?
<v Lina Misitzis>Harley 是个糟糕的歌手，但她显然有某种东西。她是个好演员。你在那个 Barbara Walters 特辑中没发现的是，她实际上继续了相当成功的童星职业生涯。她出演了一部 Nick at Nite 短片，多部商业广告，甚至一部 Val Kilmer 的电影。但我呢？

00:55:41.400 --> 00:55:44.970
<v Lina Misitzis>So OK, Mama. Be honest, OK?
<v Lina Misitzis>所以 OK，妈。老实点，好吗？

00:55:44.970 --> 00:55:47.100
<v Ira Glass>I called my mom in Greece to ask.
<v Ira Glass>我给在希腊的妈妈打电话问。

00:55:47.100 --> 00:55:51.200
<v Lina Misitzis>Was I talented?
<v Lina Misitzis>我有天赋吗？

00:55:51.200 --> 00:56:08.070
<v Woman>You were-- you had talent. But you know, I don't believe you were the star, the big star. You never got the leading role.
<v Woman>你——你有天赋。但你知道，我不相信你是明星，那个大明星。你从来没得到过主角。

00:56:08.070 --> 00:56:17.150
<v Lina Misitzis>Actually, I, like, often didn't even get in at all. And so I don't really understand why I held on to that dream for too long.
<v Lina Misitzis>实际上，我经常甚至根本进不去。所以我真的不明白为什么我太久地抓着那个梦想不放。

00:56:17.150 --> 00:56:43.720
<v Woman>You loved it, Lina. You loved it. I mean, you loved the musicals. I mean, you were like an encyclopedia. I have a very vivid memory of you going to Borders on your tippy-toes, asking the person behind the desk for the musical "Annie Get Your Gun." And you were telling him you want the 1938. No, it wasn't '38, because in '39 Judy Garland did "Wizard Of Oz."
<v Woman>你爱它，Lina。你爱它。我是说，你爱音乐剧。我是说，你就像一本百科全书。我有一个非常深刻的记忆，是你踮着脚尖走到 Borders，向柜台后面的人询问音乐剧《飞燕金枪》。你告诉他你要 1938 年的。不，那不是 38 年，因为 39 年 Judy Garland 演了《绿野仙踪》。

00:56:43.720 --> 00:57:02.280
<v Lina Misitzis>OK. So I had different talents. Harley says that when acting stopped being fun, she moved on. She's a teacher now in New Jersey. And of course, I've moved on, too. Mostly. Almost entirely. Really, any day now.
<v Lina Misitzis>好吧。所以我确实有不同的天赋。Harley 说当演戏不再有趣时，她就继续前进了。她现在是新泽西州的一名教师。当然，我也继续前进了。大多数时候。几乎完全。真的，任何时候都可以。

00:57:02.280 --> 00:57:10.850
<v Lina Misitzis>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Lina Misitzis>[音乐播放]

00:57:10.850 --> 00:57:14.490
<v Ira Glass>Lina Misitzis is one of the stars of our show.
<v Ira Glass>Lina Misitzis 是我们节目的明星之一。

00:57:14.490 --> 00:57:43.560
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - PRINCE, "I'M A STAR"]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - PRINCE, "I'M A STAR"]

